<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.5.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00929</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.5.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>569b8774-7792-4f33-83ba-546b8a05f3df</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise data-content-id="1b355614-2928-47aa-bdb9-b75be9a3c5e5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Rule \(C\) gives the monthly cost, in dollars, of using \(g\) gigabytes of data on Company \(C\)’s plan. Rule \(D\) gives the monthly cost, in dollars, of using \(g\) gigabytes of data on Company \(D\)’s plan.</para>
            <para>The total monthly cost of a cell phone plan from Company \(C\) costs $10, plus $15 per gigabyte for data used. The total monthly cost from Company \(D\) costs $80 with unlimited data.</para>
            <para>Which sentence describes the meaning of the statement \(C(2)=40\)?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>It costs $40 to use 2 gigabytes of data using plan \(C\).</para>
            <para>It costs $40 to use 2 gigabytes of data using plan \(D\).</para>
            <para>It costs $2 to use 40 gigabytes of data using plan \(C\).</para>
            <para>It costs $2 to use 40 gigabytes of data using plan \(D\).</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! It costs $40 to use 2 gigabytes of data using plan \(C\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.5.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is It costs $40 to use 2 gigabytes of data using plan \(C\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="6820dc36-de9f-4726-9ff6-1a89a1be91be" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which is less, \(C(4)\)or \(D(4)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(C(4)\) is less. \(C(4)=70\) and \(D(4)=80\). The phone plan from Company C is less expensive if 4 gigabytes of data are used.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.5.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(C(4)\) is less. \(C(4)=70\) and \(D(4)=80\). The phone plan from Company C is less expensive if 4 gigabytes of data are used. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="d5ae485c-d5e3-4d18-938b-dcfc65de291c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which is less, \(C(5)\) or \(D(5)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(D(5)\) is less. \(D(5)=80\) and \(C(5) =85\). The phone plan from Company D is less expensive if 5 gigabytes of data are used.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.5.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(D(5)\) is less. \(D(5)=80\) and \(C(5) =85\). The phone plan from Company D is less expensive if 5 gigabytes of data are used. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="01b2e207-fdc0-486e-a5af-d90331a0dde9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>For what number \(g\) is \(C(g)=130\)? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.5.3 – 4.5.5. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is 8. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="459a1487-e456-4175-987e-b7a08d4000af" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Function \(G\) takes a student’s first name for its input and gives the number of letters in the first name for its output.</para>
            <para>Find the value of \(G(\text{Jada})\). </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The name “Jada” has 4 letters.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.5.5. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is the name “Jada” has 4 letters. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="50204151-83a1-409c-90d5-d32808ffbaa5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Find the value of \(G(\text{Diego})\).</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.5.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is 5. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="de4c36d3-4c57-41cc-8605-d9865feb2be0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Is the following graph a function?</para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="The figure has an absolute value function graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The \(x\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The \(y\)-axis runs from negative 4 to 8. The vertex is at the point (0, negative 1). The line goes through the points (negative 1, 0) and (1, 0).">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_08_13_01.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Yes, this is a function because the graph passes the vertical line test.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.5.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Yes, this is a function because the graph passes the vertical line test. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="d546abc7-2562-40a8-bc3c-7d3e74c54ded" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Is the following graph a function?</para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="The figure has a half-circle graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The \(x\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The \(y\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The curved line segment starts at the point (negative 2, 0). The line goes through the point (0, 2) and ends at the point (2, 0).">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_08_13_02.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Yes, this is a function because the graph passes the vertical line test.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.5.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Yes, this is a function because the graph passes the vertical line test. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="ff99ea32-dcd7-4674-8b3b-6b1e0ee78860" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Is the following graph a function?</para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="The figure has two curved lines graphed on the x y-coordinate plane. The \(x\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The \(y\)-axis runs from negative 6 to 6. The curved line on the left goes through the points (negative 2, 0), (negative 4, 5), and (negative 4, negative 5). The curved line on the right goes through the points (2, 0), (4, 5), and (4, negative 5).">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_08_13_03.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! No, this is not a function because the graph does not pass the vertical line test.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.5.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: No, this is not a function because the graph does not pass the vertical line test. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
